const editorialExtras={"huffer": "<div class=\"editorial-story\"><div class=\"editorial-intro\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CURATED PROCESS / DESIGN DECISIONS</span><h3>The story behind the work.</h3><p>A closer look at the documented decisions and changes, with original source pages preserved below.</p></div><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">01 / PROJECT ORIGIN</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Freedom after routine</h3><p>The campaign began with a contrast: school and work shape everyday routines, while the hours afterwards offer young people space to express themselves. This became the core message of Huffer Time, a conceptual campaign for a New Zealand streetwear label.</p><div class=\"story-image-pair\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-02.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 2\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 02</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">02 / VISUAL SYSTEM</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Creating a recognisable language</h3><p>The visual identity relies on strong typography, an orange-to-pink-to-purple gradient and urban imagery. The system aims to stay recognisable while adapting to different products rather than imposing one fixed layout.</p><div class=\"story-image-pair\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-13.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 13\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 13</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">03 / DIGITAL EXPERIENCE</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Designing a complete store</h3><p>The PDF records a homepage, collection browsing, product detail, About page and cart. The work needed strong campaign visuals as well as clear categories, filters, product information and checkout hierarchy.</p><div class=\"story-image-pair\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-03.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 3\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 03</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-04.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 4\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 04</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-05.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 5\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 05</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-06.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 6\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 06</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-07.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 7\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 07</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-08.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 8\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 08</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">04 / PHYSICAL PACKAGING</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Taking identity off-screen</h3><p>The packaging uses a folding clothing box, with graphic elements continuing across the exterior panels. Prototype and lifestyle images show how the box relates to the clothing and creates a more considered unboxing experience.</p><div class=\"story-image-pair\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-09.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 9\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 09</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-10.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 10\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 10</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">05 / ADVERTISING</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>A message at poster scale</h3><p>The promotional poster combines an oversized Huffer Time headline with skateboarding imagery. The document shows both the poster itself and applications in a retail environment and bus-stop setting.</p><div class=\"story-image-pair\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-11.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 11\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 11</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-12.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 12\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 12</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">06 / REFINEMENT</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Boldness needs restraint</h3><p>The reflection records the challenge of making typography, gradients and photography work without competing for attention. Simplifying crowded layouts and adjusting the identity for different media became central design decisions.</p><div class=\"story-image-pair\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-14.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 14\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 14</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-15.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 15\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 15</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">07 / FINAL DOCUMENTATION</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>The complete 16-page case study</h3><p>The source PDF preserves the project from its opening concept through digital screens, packaging, advertising, consistent visual identity, design decisions and reflection. Every page follows in its original sequence below.</p><div class=\"story-image-pair\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-01.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 1\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 01</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/huffer-full-16.webp\" alt=\"Huffer Time original page 16\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 16</figcaption></figure></div></div></section></div>", "move": "<div class=\"editorial-story\"><div class=\"editorial-intro\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CURATED PROCESS / DESIGN DECISIONS</span><h3>The story behind the work.</h3><p>A closer look at the documented decisions and changes, with original source pages preserved below.</p></div><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">01 / WHAT IF</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Can comfort become a problem?</h3><p>MOVE is a DES232 group smart-sofa concept investigating prolonged sitting and the tension between gentle support and unwanted control. The design asks whether responsive furniture might make sedentary habits more noticeable.</p></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">02 / EARLY EXPLORATION</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>From paper to an initial form</h3><p>The Miro board documents references, questions, moodboards and a fast paper-prototype sprint. The team explored sofa shape and the positioning of possible smart components before building a more detailed physical structure.</p></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">03 / PHYSICAL BUILD</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Framing and upholstery</h3><p>The team developed a wooden support structure and then added padding and fabric covering. Nicky contributed to sofa framing, stitching, upholstery and making room for the planned sensor and electronic components.</p></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">04 / INTERACTION &amp; LIMITS</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Balancing care with choice</h3><p>The proposed experience pairs sitting-duration sensing with subtle movement or other feedback and a pause or rest option. The group records that sensing and automatic movement would require independent verification; the physical prototype chiefly demonstrates form and assembly.</p></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">05 / TEAM REFLECTION</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>A shared prototype</h3><p>Further work includes a product story and demonstration video. The project shows the practical difficulty of connecting product form, component clearance, user control and a meaningful demonstration of interaction.</p></div></section></div>"};
editorialExtras.move="<div class=\"editorial-story project-process-v11\"><div class=\"editorial-intro\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">PROCESS STUDY / RESEARCH → IDEATION → PROTOTYPING → REFLECTION</span><h3>From question to outcome.</h3><p>Follow the decisions, experiments and evidence that shaped the project.</p></div><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">01 / THE QUESTION</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>When does comfort become too much?</h3><p>MOVE began with an uncomfortable design question: could a sofa make long periods of sitting noticeable while still respecting the user’s right to rest? Rather than adding more phone alerts, the team explored feedback coming from the furniture itself.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid single\"><figure><img src=\"assets/move-full-01.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"When does comfort become too much? — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 01 · 01</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">02 / CONTEXT & PRECEDENTS</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Looking beyond passive notifications</h3><p>The group reviewed ideas such as responsive furniture, gesture-controlled adjustable seating and behaviour-change products. These examples made the team consider small physical feedback, clear user control and the risk of interventions becoming intrusive. The intended behaviour was a gentle invitation to notice posture or take a break, not a forced command.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid single\"><figure><img src=\"assets/move-full-01.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Looking beyond passive notifications — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 02 · 01</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">03 / INITIAL PROTOTYPE</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Making the idea tangible with paper</h3><p>A low-fidelity paper and cardboard sofa model was used to explore the basic form, possible moving parts, and how the user might understand the proposed response. It did not contain working sensors. Early feedback highlighted the need for clearer proportions, a more realistic structure and a better explanation of how the functions would fit inside.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid single\"><figure><img src=\"assets/move-board.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Making the idea tangible with paper — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 03 · 01</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">04 / STRUCTURAL DEVELOPMENT</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>From a paper study to a wooden frame</h3><p>The next stage focused on a small-scale wooden support structure. The team planned the seat, backrest and arms while checking space for internal mechanisms and access to sensors or electronics. In my own work I helped assemble the sofa frame, turning concept sketches into a construction that could support the later upholstery.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid \"><figure><img src=\"assets/move-prototype.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"From a paper study to a wooden frame — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 04 · 01</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"assets/move-build.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"From a paper study to a wooden frame — documented project image 2\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 04 · 02</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">05 / MATERIAL EXPERIMENTS</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Padding, covering and stitching</h3><p>I then worked on the soft finish, including cushioning, fabric covering and hand stitching. At the same time, I worked with teammates to leave adequate room for the intended sensing and electronic components. The upholstered form helped the miniature prototype communicate “sofa” more clearly, but also made clear that comfort and repair access require further testing.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid \"><figure><img src=\"assets/move-build.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Padding, covering and stitching — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 05 · 01</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"assets/move-final.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Padding, covering and stitching — documented project image 2\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 05 · 02</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">06 / INTERACTION & ETHICS</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>A response the user can decline</h3><p>MOVE imagines detecting extended sitting and responding through subtle seat or backrest adjustments, with settings or Rest Mode allowing users to pause the intervention. The central design debate became as important as the object itself: when does assistance cross into control? The prototype and story communicate this intention; reliable sensor behaviour and automated movement were not established as fully tested.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid single\"><figure><img src=\"assets/move-full-02.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"A response the user can decline — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 06 · 01</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">07 / COMMUNICATION</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Building the demonstration story</h3><p>For the final communication work I contributed ideas for the promotional-video script and content, gave feedback on the sequence and helped the team refine its message. The storyboard had to show the everyday sitting problem, the proposed feedback and the moment when the user can choose what happens next.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid single\"><figure><img src=\"assets/move-full-02.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Building the demonstration story — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 07 · 01</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">08 / REFLECTION</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>What building changed</h3><p>Physical making revealed questions that were easy to miss in an early concept: frame stability, component clearance, fabric access and whether an intervention would be understandable rather than annoying. The most important lesson for me was to think about interaction and construction together from an earlier stage, while being honest about what the prototype actually demonstrates.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid single\"><figure><img src=\"assets/move-final.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"What building changed — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 08 · 01</figcaption></figure></div></div></section></div>";
editorialExtras.roadpal="<div class=\"editorial-story project-process-v11\"><div class=\"editorial-intro\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">PROCESS STUDY / RESEARCH → IDEATION → PROTOTYPING → REFLECTION</span><h3>From question to outcome.</h3><p>Follow the decisions, experiments and evidence that shaped the project.</p></div><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">01 / PROJECT OPPORTUNITY</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>From online inspiration to shared experiences</h3><p>Road Pal was developed around the relationship between travel, digital socialisation and meeting people in person. The group proposed a platform where users discover trips through shared posts, explore itineraries, connect with travellers and organise a real-world experience. The DES220 submission frames this as bringing nature and like-minded people into a more socially connected travel app.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid \"><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-01.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"From online inspiration to shared experiences — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 01 · 01</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-02.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"From online inspiration to shared experiences — documented project image 2\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 01 · 02</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">02 / LOW-FIDELITY IDEATION</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Starting with movable paper screens</h3><p>Before using Figma, the team made a paper-based interaction diagram. A phone-shaped frame could move across different page sketches to simulate navigation. This provided a quick way to discuss sequence and screen relationships without building the digital interface first.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid single\"><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-02.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Starting with movable paper screens — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 02 · 01</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">03 / TOOL & PROCESS DECISION</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Why the team moved to Figma</h3><p>The team switched to Figma after early feedback. The submitted rationale identifies practical reasons: faster editing, lower material cost, easier collaboration and previous experience with the software. The move made it possible to compare multiple layouts, visual styles and navigation options in one evolving prototype.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid \"><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-02.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Why the team moved to Figma — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 03 · 01</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-03.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Why the team moved to Figma — documented project image 2\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 03 · 02</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">04 / WIREFRAMES & ITERATIONS</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Finding a coherent interface</h3><p>Screens were iterated across the trip feed, exploration, messaging, trip planning and invitation experience. The team compared layouts, photographs, hierarchy and interaction connections before converging on the final green-and-white UI. The submission includes both early experiments and a final Figma screen overview.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid \"><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-03.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Finding a coherent interface — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 04 · 01</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-08.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Finding a coherent interface — documented project image 2\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 04 · 02</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">05 / CORE USER JOURNEY</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Discover → Connect → Plan → Go</h3><p>A user can browse travel posts for ideas, interact with content, explore trip details and use the planning interface to form a trip. The concept also invites friends or fellow travellers, helping translate online discovery into a shared outing. The screens illustrate features for travel preferences, companions and trip arrangements; they are prototype flows rather than a connected booking backend.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid \"><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-04.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Discover → Connect → Plan → Go — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 05 · 01</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-08.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Discover → Connect → Plan → Go — documented project image 2\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 05 · 02</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">06 / MY PRACTICE IN THE TEAM</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Discussion, visuals and Figma execution</h3><p>My individual contribution focused on proposing ideas, refining them in discussion with teammates, creating and iterating visual options, and implementing agreed interface work in Figma. I was particularly interested in making the social dimension visible, so the app would support meeting people instead of becoming only a trip-booking service. The final artefact remains collaborative.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid \"><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-03.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Discussion, visuals and Figma execution — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 06 · 01</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-08.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Discussion, visuals and Figma execution — documented project image 2\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 06 · 02</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">07 / COMMUNICATION MATERIALS</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Taking the concept beyond the screen</h3><p>The group used an A2-style poster with a View → Plan → Go explanation and showed the prototype in a promotional video. Exhibition materials included a flyer, stickers, a guestbook-style booklet and a green display surface. These helped visitors understand the app and interact with the identity without needing a fully functioning production service.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid \"><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-04.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Taking the concept beyond the screen — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 07 · 01</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-05.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Taking the concept beyond the screen — documented project image 2\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 07 · 02</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">08 / EXHIBITION & LEARNING</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Testing how people understand the idea</h3><p>The final exhibition presented a tablet-based prototype, poster, printed materials and demonstration. The photographs document classmates trying the app and the team communicating the concept. The submission does not provide quantified user-testing results, so this page treats the exhibition as documented demonstration rather than claiming measured impact. For me, the key lesson was connecting a visually clear interface to a social purpose that visitors could quickly understand.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid single\"><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-06.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Testing how people understand the idea — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 08 · 01</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">09 / FINAL SCREENS</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>A complete visual account of the proposal</h3><p>The final presentation brings the interface screens together: onboarding, discovery, social connections, travel posts, creating a trip, adding people and confirmation. The entire eight-page source PDF follows below so the complete submitted design record is accessible.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid single\"><figure><img src=\"assets/roadpal-full-08.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"A complete visual account of the proposal — documented project image 1\"><figcaption>PROCESS EVIDENCE / 09 · 01</figcaption></figure></div></div></section></div>";
editorialExtras.shoulder="<div class=\"editorial-story project-process-v11\"><div class=\"editorial-intro\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">ORIGINAL TATTOO DESIGN PRACTICE / SEPTEMBER 2026</span><h3>From line experiments to body placement.</h3><p>Follow the actual documented sessions, sketches, revisions and placement studies behind the final jellyfish design.</p></div><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">01 / PRACTICE REVIEW</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Learning to work with intention</h3><p>Before developing the final design, I reviewed my working habits. I noticed that I could spend too long collecting references or refining small details without a clear goal. I began separating research, sketching, selection and refinement, and started evaluating composition as a whole rather than adding details to solve every problem.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid \"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-02.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 2\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 02</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-03.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 3\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 03</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-04.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 4\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 04</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">02 / EARLY LINE STUDIES</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Building confidence in flowing lines</h3><p>On 14 September 2026, I spent 1.5 hours making small stream-of-consciousness sketches. I practised brush control and the way curves connect. The initial marks sometimes felt too heavy and crowded; reducing line thickness and allowing more negative space made the drawings cleaner.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid single\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-06.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 6\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 06</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">03 / RESEARCHING TECHNIQUES</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Looking beyond a single tattoo style</h3><p>On 18 September, I studied contrasting approaches to shading, dotwork, bold outlines and broken lines. Instead of forcing two complete styles together, I learned to select useful techniques based on line weight, texture, visual focus and the needs of each drawing.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid single\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-07.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 7\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 07</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">04 / OBSERVATION &amp; STUDY</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Understanding structure before detail</h3><p>On 22 September, I made studies based on existing tattoo references. Copying them revealed how easy it was to lose proportion by focusing on individual lines too soon. I began paying more attention to the overall shape, spacing, line direction and hierarchy before refining smaller marks. These studies were for learning, not claimed as original artwork.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid single\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-08.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 8\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 08</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">05 / FIRST THEMED EXPERIMENT</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>From abstract lines to a butterfly</h3><p>On 24 September, I moved beyond purely abstract sketches and tried an animal theme. I explored reference images, developed a butterfly-inspired form and asked my family for feedback. They felt that some realistic details overwhelmed the loose linework. I simplified the recognisable features and used flowing extensions to suggest movement while preserving the theme.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid \"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-09.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 9\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 09</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-10.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 10\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 10</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">06 / JELLYFISH CONCEPT</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Choosing a subject that naturally moves</h3><p>On 29 September, I chose a jellyfish because its tentacles already suggest fluid motion. This made it possible to combine a recognisable subject with the flowing line language I had been developing. I began exploring its placement on a back rather than designing only for a flat page.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid \"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-11.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 11\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 11</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-12.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 12\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 12</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">07 / ITERATION &amp; LINE DENSITY</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Balancing detail, movement and restraint</h3><p>At first I worried that extending the tentacles would make the composition messy. I tested several arrangements and gradually allowed the lines to spread further. This created stronger movement. I also explored closely spaced fine lines to build shadow and depth, discovering that controlled density could add dimension without filling every area.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid single\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-12.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 12\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 12</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">08 / PLACEMENT TESTS</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Designing with the body, not over it</h3><p>The most difficult decision was where the jellyfish should sit. Its position needed to be established before the tentacles could be directed along the shoulder and back. On 30 September, I spent one hour testing scale and placement using body-outline images. I compared upper-back, central and shoulder arrangements and refined the design so its movement worked with the silhouette.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid \"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-13.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 13\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 13</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-14.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 14\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 14</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">09 / FINAL OUTCOME</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>Body &amp; Linework — a placement study</h3><p>The final presentation places the jellyfish across the shoulder and upper back. Its main form anchors the design while long curves follow the body and create an open, dynamic composition. This is a digital tattoo-placement study on a body illustration, not a photograph of an inked tattoo.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid single\"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-15.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 15\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 15</figcaption></figure></div></div></section><section class=\"story-stage\"><div class=\"story-number\">10 / REFLECTION &amp; NEXT STEPS</div><div class=\"story-content\"><h3>What changed in my practice</h3><p>This process moved my attention from making individual lines attractive to designing their relationship with a theme, negative space and a specific body location. I also learned to start with a clearer aim, use feedback without losing my own expression, and test scale and placement earlier. The original fifteen-page DES221 practice document is available below.</p><div class=\"stage-image-grid \"><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-02.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 2\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 02</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-03.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 3\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 03</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-04.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 4\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 04</figcaption></figure><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/body-process-15.webp\" alt=\"Body & Linework practice, original PDF page 15\"><figcaption>DOCUMENTED PROCESS / PAGE 15</figcaption></figure></div></div></section></div>";
